      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-z$ that passes through the point $P(0,-2,4).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2,4) & ={2}^{2}-{0}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =4-0-4 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-z & =0 \\\\ z & ={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(0,-2,4)$ is the hyperbolic paraboloid $z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
